IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `hpr-law`

-- Health Practitioner Regulation (Adoption of National Law) Act 2009 (NSW) -- the questions of the person who
-- complains about a registered health practitioner in New South Wales.
--
-- SEAT: the complainant -- a patient hurt or let down by a doctor, nurse, dentist, pharmacist or other registered
-- practitioner, or a family member or anyone else (s 144B: "Any person can make a complaint"), often weighing or bringing
-- a civil claim over the same treatment. Every question is one that person would ask: "Can I complain?", "Where do I
-- lodge it?", "Must it go to the Tribunal?", "When must the Council tell me the outcome?", "Can I have a lawyer?", "Is
-- my appeal in time?", "What can happen to the practitioner?", "Can I get my fees back?", "Can I be made to pay costs?",
-- "Can this finding be used in my claim?". The Councils, the Health Care Complaints Commission, the Committees and the
-- Tribunal get no seat: what they must or may do is encoded as what it means for the complainant.
--
-- The rules shared with the practitioner's seat are in hpr-law.l4; this module asks the complainant's questions and
-- holds the provisions only the complainant meets. What is out, and why, is in NOTES.md.
--
-- Based on content from the New South Wales Legislation website (as collected by the Open Australian Legal Corpus on
-- 10 March 2025; unofficial, possibly out of date). CC BY 4.0. BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `The complainant's questions`

§§ `The complainant's nouns`

-- ss 139G, 139H: whether, and under what, the practitioner is or was registered.
DECLARE `Practitioner's registration` IS ONE OF
    `registered under the National Law now`
    `formerly registered under the National Law`
    `registered only under a corresponding prior Act, never under the National Law`
    `never registered`

-- s 144: the grounds of complaint about a registered health practitioner.
DECLARE `Ground of complaint` IS ONE OF
    `a criminal conviction or criminal finding for an offence`
    `unsatisfactory professional conduct or professional misconduct`
    `not competent to practise the profession`
    `an impairment`
    `otherwise not a suitable person to hold registration`
    `something else`

DECLARE `Can I complain` IS ONE OF
    `yes: any person can make this complaint`
    `yes, about the practitioner's behaviour while registered`
    `yes, about behaviour while registered under the prior Act, to the extent that Act allowed`
    `no: the prior Act would not have allowed this complaint`
    `the Act does not provide for this complaint on these facts`

-- s 144C: where a complaint may be made.
DECLARE `Where I complain` IS ONE OF
    `a Council`
    `the Health Care Complaints Commission`

DECLARE `How to lodge` IS ONE OF
    `lodge it with the Council's Executive Officer: it can be referred once lodged`
    `lodge it with the Commission under s 9 of the Health Care Complaints Act 1993: it can be referred once lodged`
    `lodge it with the Council's Executive Officer: it may be looked into, but cannot be referred until it is in writing with the particulars of the allegations`
    `lodge it with the Commission under s 9 of the Health Care Complaints Act 1993: it may be looked into, but cannot be referred until it is in writing with the particulars of the allegations`

DECLARE `Referral of my complaint` IS ONE OF
    `no: the practitioner has died`
    `not yet: it must first be in writing with the particulars of the allegations`
    `it may be referred, but need not be: the further particulars asked for were not given`
    `it may be referred, but need not be: the practitioner is no longer registered`
    `yes, even though civil or criminal proceedings on the same matter are under way`
    `yes`

DECLARE `Who holds my complaint` IS ONE OF
    `the Council`
    `the Commission`

DECLARE `Going to the Tribunal` IS ONE OF
    `section 145D does not require it to go to the Tribunal`
    `not required: the Council or the Commission thinks it frivolous or vexatious`
    `the Council may send it instead to a Committee or an Impaired Registrants Panel`
    `the Commission may send it instead to the Council`
    `it must be referred to the Tribunal`

-- s 145B(1) and s 145BA(1): what the Council did with the complaint.
DECLARE `What the Council did` IS ONE OF
    `dealt with it by inquiry at a Council meeting`
    `took action after referring the practitioner for a health or performance assessment or to an Impaired Registrants Panel`
    `directed the practitioner to attend counselling`
    `referred it to the Commission for conciliation, or under Division 9 of Part 2 of the Health Care Complaints Act 1993`
    `referred it to another entity, such as a National Board`
    `decided that no further action should be taken`
    `made inquiries about it`
    `referred it to the Commission for investigation`
    `referred it to the Tribunal`
    `referred it to a Committee`

DECLARE `Outcome notice` IS ONE OF
    `the Council must give me written notice of the outcome by`
        HAS `the last day` IS A DATE
    `the Council must give me written notice of the result, and a written statement of the decision with reasons, by`
        HAS `the last day for both` IS A DATE
    `section 145BA does not require the Council to tell me of this step`

DECLARE `Outcome notice timing` IS ONE OF
    `given in time`
    `given late: more than 30 days after the outcome`
    `not yet given, and not yet due`
    `not yet given, and overdue`
    `section 145BA does not require notice of this step`

DECLARE `Suspension or cancellation` IS ONE OF
    `the Tribunal must cancel the registration`
    `the Tribunal may suspend or cancel the registration`
    `a Committee or the Council may recommend suspension or cancellation to the Tribunal`
    `the Act gives no power to suspend or cancel on these findings`

DECLARE `Document from the complaint` IS ONE OF
    `a finding of a Council at an inquiry at a Council meeting`
    `advice an Assessment Committee obtained`
    `a skills-testing report to an Assessment Committee`
    `the Council's written reasons for urgent suspension or conditions (s 150)`
    `the Council's written reasons on a review of urgent action (s 150A)`
    `an audio recording or transcript of a Council meeting about urgent action (s 150B)`
    `another document or finding`

DECLARE `Use in my civil claim` IS ONE OF
    `admissible as evidence in any legal proceedings`
    `not admissible in my civil claim, and no one can be made to produce it or give evidence about it`
    `usable, because the Council has ordered otherwise`
    `no one can be required to produce it or reveal its contents in civil proceedings`
    `not admissible in court proceedings (other than under the National Law) or at an inquest`
    `the Act does not say`

§§ `Making a complaint -- ss 139G, 139H, 144, 144B-144E`

-- s 144: "The following complaints may be made about a registered health practitioner -- (a) ... convicted of or made
-- the subject of a criminal finding for an offence; (b) ... guilty of unsatisfactory professional conduct or
-- professional misconduct; (c) ... not competent ...; (d) ... has an impairment; (e) ... otherwise not a suitable person
-- to hold registration". s 144B(1): "Any person can make a complaint." s 139G: a person no longer registered under the
-- National Law may be complained about "in relation to the person's behaviour while registered". s 139H(3): for a
-- person registered only under a corresponding prior Act, "only to the extent (a) a notification about the person's
-- behaviour could have been made under the corresponding prior Act". The list of grounds is not closed with "only":
-- a complaint on no listed ground, or about someone never registered, is "not provided for", not a firm no.
@export Can I make a complaint about this health practitioner in New South Wales (ss 139G, 139H, 144, 144B)?
GIVEN `the registration` IS A `Practitioner's registration` @desc The practitioner's registration. One of: "registered under the National Law now", "formerly registered under the National Law", "registered only under a corresponding prior Act, never under the National Law" (registered under an earlier NSW Act such as the Medical Practice Act 1992 but not since 2010), "never registered"
      `the ground` IS A `Ground of complaint` @desc What you say about the practitioner. One of: "a criminal conviction or criminal finding for an offence", "unsatisfactory professional conduct or professional misconduct", "not competent to practise the profession", "an impairment", "otherwise not a suitable person to hold registration", "something else"
      `it is about behaviour while registered` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If the practitioner is no longer registered: whether your complaint is about what they did while they were registered
      `the prior Act allowed such a complaint` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If the practitioner was registered only under an earlier Act: whether a complaint about this behaviour could have been made under that earlier Act
GIVETH A `Can I complain`
`Can I make a complaint` MEANS
    IF `the ground` EQUALS `something else`
    THEN `the Act does not provide for this complaint on these facts`
    ELSE CONSIDER `the registration`
         WHEN `registered under the National Law now` THEN `yes: any person can make this complaint`
         WHEN `formerly registered under the National Law` THEN
             IF `it is about behaviour while registered`
             THEN `yes, about the practitioner's behaviour while registered`
             ELSE `the Act does not provide for this complaint on these facts`
         WHEN `registered only under a corresponding prior Act, never under the National Law` THEN
             IF (NOT `it is about behaviour while registered`)
             THEN `the Act does not provide for this complaint on these facts`
             ELSE IF `the prior Act allowed such a complaint`
                  THEN `yes, about behaviour while registered under the prior Act, to the extent that Act allowed`
                  ELSE `no: the prior Act would not have allowed this complaint`
         WHEN `never registered` THEN `the Act does not provide for this complaint on these facts`

-- s 144C: "A complaint may be made to a Council or the Commission." s 144D(1): a complaint (other than by a Council or
-- the Secretary) "must (a) be in writing; and (b) contain particulars of the allegations on which it is founded"; (2)
-- it "need not be made in terms that are strictly consistent with the terminology of section 144"; (3) a Council or the
-- Commission "may consider and investigate a complaint that does not comply ... but must not refer the complaint ...
-- until the requirements are complied with". s 144E: to a Council, "lodged with the Executive Officer of the Council";
-- to the Commission, "in accordance with section 9 of the Health Care Complaints Act 1993".
@export Where and how do I lodge my complaint about a health practitioner, and is it in a form that can be referred for a hearing (ss 144C-144E)?
GIVEN `where I complain` IS A `Where I complain` @desc Where you are complaining. One of: "a Council" (the NSW council for the practitioner's profession, e.g. the Medical Council of NSW), "the Health Care Complaints Commission"
      `it is in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your complaint is in writing
      `it gives the particulars of my allegations` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your complaint sets out the particulars of what you allege (it need not use the Act's terms)
GIVETH A `How to lodge`
`How do I lodge my complaint` MEANS
    IF `it is in writing` AND `it gives the particulars of my allegations`
    THEN IF `where I complain` EQUALS `a Council`
         THEN `lodge it with the Council's Executive Officer: it can be referred once lodged`
         ELSE `lodge it with the Commission under s 9 of the Health Care Complaints Act 1993: it can be referred once lodged`
    ELSE IF `where I complain` EQUALS `a Council`
         THEN `lodge it with the Council's Executive Officer: it may be looked into, but cannot be referred until it is in writing with the particulars of the allegations`
         ELSE `lodge it with the Commission under s 9 of the Health Care Complaints Act 1993: it may be looked into, but cannot be referred until it is in writing with the particulars of the allegations`

§§ `What the practitioner did -- ss 139, 139B-139E`

@export Could what the practitioner did be unsatisfactory professional conduct under the NSW law (ss 139B, 139C, 139D)? Answers yes, or "not shown" where it meets no head the Act lists (the deciding body may still find it improper or unethical).
GIVEN `the profession` IS A `Health profession` @desc The practitioner's profession. One of: "medical", "nursing and midwifery", "pharmacy", "another registered health profession" (dentist, psychologist, physiotherapist, paramedic and so on)
      `the conduct` IS A `Conduct complained of` @desc What the practitioner did; pick the first that fits. One of the heads in ss 139B(1), 139C and 139D, from "care, skill or judgment significantly below the standard reasonably expected of an equivalent practitioner" to "none of these"
      `the provider` IS A `Kind of provider` @desc If it was a referral or recommendation: who it was to. One of: "a public company", "a private company", "a natural person", "another kind of provider or supplier"
      `percentage of issued shares held` IS A NUMBER @desc If a public company: the percentage of its issued share capital the practitioner holds (e.g. 5 for 5%; 0 if none or unknown)
      `has any interest in the private company` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If a private company: whether the practitioner has any interest in it
      `the provider is the practitioner's partner` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If a natural person: whether that person is the practitioner's partner
      `a prescribed circumstance applies` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the NSW regulations prescribe the circumstances as giving the practitioner a financial interest (the regulations are not encoded)
      `disclosed the interest before or at the time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the practitioner told you the nature of their interest before or when making the referral or recommendation
      `took all reasonable steps to have another doctor attend` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If a doctor failed to attend urgently: whether the doctor took all reasonable steps to make sure another doctor attended within a reasonable time
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Could it be unsatisfactory professional conduct` MEANS
    `is unsatisfactory professional conduct` `the profession` `the conduct`
        (`has a financial interest` `the provider` `percentage of issued shares held` `has any interest in the private company` `the provider is the practitioner's partner` `a prescribed circumstance applies`)
        `disclosed the interest before or at the time`
        `took all reasonable steps to have another doctor attend`

@export Could what the practitioner did be professional misconduct, the serious kind that can lead to suspension or cancellation (s 139E)?
GIVEN `the profession` IS A `Health profession` @desc The practitioner's profession. One of: "medical", "nursing and midwifery", "pharmacy", "another registered health profession"
      `the conduct` IS A `Conduct complained of` @desc What the practitioner did; pick the first that fits (the heads in ss 139B(1), 139C, 139D, or "none of these")
      `disclosed the interest before or at the time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If it was a referral or recommendation in which the practitioner had a financial interest (s 139B(2)): whether they told you of it before or at the time
      `the practitioner had a financial interest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If it was a referral or recommendation: whether the practitioner had a financial interest in it as s 139B(2) defines (5% or more of a public company, any interest in a private company, a partner, or a prescribed circumstance)
      `took all reasonable steps to have another doctor attend` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If a doctor failed to attend urgently: whether the doctor took all reasonable steps to have another doctor attend
      `serious enough to justify suspension or cancellation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal finds the conduct (alone, or several instances taken together) serious enough to justify suspending or cancelling the registration
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Could it be professional misconduct` MEANS
    `is professional misconduct`
        (`is unsatisfactory professional conduct` `the profession` `the conduct` (JUST `the practitioner had a financial interest`) `disclosed the interest before or at the time` `took all reasonable steps to have another doctor attend`)
        `serious enough to justify suspension or cancellation`

@export Is the practitioner competent to practise, as the Act defines it (s 139)?
GIVEN `sufficient physical capacity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the deciding body finds the practitioner has sufficient physical capacity to practise
      `sufficient mental capacity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the deciding body finds the practitioner has sufficient mental capacity to practise
      `sufficient knowledge and skill` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the deciding body finds the practitioner has sufficient knowledge and skill to practise
      `sufficient communication skills, including English` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the deciding body finds the practitioner has sufficient communication skills, including an adequate command of English
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is the practitioner competent to practise` MEANS
    `competent to practise` `sufficient physical capacity` `sufficient mental capacity` `sufficient knowledge and skill` `sufficient communication skills, including English`

§§ `How the complaint is dealt with -- ss 144D(3), 145B, 145D, 145G-145J, 146, 147A, 148`

-- s 145H: "A complaint is not to be referred under this Subdivision if the registered health practitioner ... is dead."
-- s 144D(3): not to be referred until in writing with particulars. s 145J: "may decide not to refer ... if the
-- complainant has failed to provide further particulars in response to a request". s 145I: "may decide not to refer ...
-- if the registered health practitioner ... has ceased to be registered". s 145G: a complaint "can be referred ... and
-- dealt with ... even though the practitioner ... is the subject of proposed or current criminal or civil proceedings
-- relating to the subject-matter of the complaint".
@export Can my complaint still be referred for a hearing -- even if I am suing the practitioner, or they have died or stopped practising (ss 144D(3), 145G-145J)?
GIVEN `the practitioner has died` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the practitioner has died
      `it is in writing with particulars` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your complaint is in writing and sets out the particulars of your allegations
      `I did not give further particulars asked for` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Council or the Commission asked you for further particulars and you did not provide them
      `the practitioner is still registered` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the practitioner is still registered
      `civil or criminal proceedings are under way` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether there are proposed or current civil proceedings (such as your own claim for damages) or criminal proceedings about the same matter
GIVETH A `Referral of my complaint`
`Can my complaint be referred for a hearing` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the practitioner has died` THEN `no: the practitioner has died`
           IF (NOT `it is in writing with particulars`) THEN `not yet: it must first be in writing with the particulars of the allegations`
           IF `I did not give further particulars asked for` THEN `it may be referred, but need not be: the further particulars asked for were not given`
           IF (NOT `the practitioner is still registered`) THEN `it may be referred, but need not be: the practitioner is no longer registered`
           IF `civil or criminal proceedings are under way` THEN `yes, even though civil or criminal proceedings on the same matter are under way`
           OTHERWISE `yes`

-- s 145D(1): a Council and the Commission "are under a duty to refer a complaint to the Tribunal if, at any time, either
-- forms the opinion that it may, if substantiated, provide grounds for the suspension or cancellation of [the]
-- registration". (2) Either may decide not to if the allegations "relate solely or principally to ... the physical or
-- mental capacity of the practitioner to practise"; (3) a Council must then refer it to a Committee or an Impaired
-- Registrants Panel; (4) the Commission must then refer it to the Council. (5) No referral is required of a complaint
-- the Council or Commission "thinks is frivolous or vexatious". The opinions are theirs, asked.
@export Must my complaint go to the Civil and Administrative Tribunal (s 145D)?
GIVEN `who holds it` IS A `Who holds my complaint` @desc Who is dealing with your complaint. One of: "the Council", "the Commission" (the Health Care Complaints Commission)
      `they think it could justify suspension or cancellation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Council or the Commission thinks the complaint, if proved, may justify suspending or cancelling the practitioner's registration
      `it is solely or principally about capacity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the allegations (with any other pending complaint) are solely or mainly about the practitioner's physical or mental capacity to practise
      `they think it frivolous or vexatious` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Council or the Commission thinks the complaint frivolous or vexatious
GIVETH A `Going to the Tribunal`
`Must my complaint go to the Tribunal` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT `they think it could justify suspension or cancellation`) THEN `section 145D does not require it to go to the Tribunal`
           IF `they think it frivolous or vexatious` THEN `not required: the Council or the Commission thinks it frivolous or vexatious`
           IF `it is solely or principally about capacity` AND `who holds it` EQUALS `the Council` THEN `the Council may send it instead to a Committee or an Impaired Registrants Panel`
           IF `it is solely or principally about capacity` THEN `the Commission may send it instead to the Council`
           OTHERWISE `it must be referred to the Tribunal`

-- s 145B(1): a Council may refer a complaint "(c) to the Tribunal; (d) to a Committee; (e) for a complaint about a
-- [practitioner] registered in a health profession other than the medical or nursing and midwifery profession, ...
-- [deal with it] by inquiry at a meeting of the Council". A Professional Standards Committee deals only with medical and
-- nursing and midwifery practitioners (ss 146, 168, 169A). s 147A(1): a Council "may refer a complaint to [an Assessment]
-- Committee only if (a) the complaint is about a [practitioner other than medical or nursing and midwifery]; and (b) the
-- Commission has decided not to investigate the complaint or following an investigation has decided not to refer the
-- complaint to the Tribunal"; (2) not if it is a complaint that the practitioner "is not of good character" or "has been
-- convicted of or made the subject of a criminal finding for an offence". s 148: a Council meeting does not deal with
-- medical or nursing and midwifery practitioners.
@export Which bodies can hear my complaint about this practitioner (ss 145B(1), 146, 147A, 148)?
GIVEN `the profession` IS A `Health profession` @desc The practitioner's profession. One of: "medical", "nursing and midwifery", "pharmacy", "another registered health profession"
      `the Commission declined to investigate or to go to the Tribunal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Health Care Complaints Commission has decided not to investigate your complaint, or investigated it and decided not to refer it to the Tribunal
      `it alleges bad character or a conviction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your complaint says the practitioner is not of good character, or has been convicted of (or found guilty of) an offence
GIVETH A LIST OF `Complaint body`
`Which bodies can hear my complaint` MEANS
    IF `is a relevant health practitioner for a Professional Standards Committee` `the profession`
    THEN LIST `the Tribunal`, `a Professional Standards Committee`
    ELSE IF `the Commission declined to investigate or to go to the Tribunal` AND (NOT `it alleges bad character or a conviction`)
         THEN LIST `the Tribunal`, `an Assessment Committee`, `the Council at an inquiry at a Council meeting`
         ELSE LIST `the Tribunal`, `the Council at an inquiry at a Council meeting`

§§ `Being told the outcome -- ss 145BA, 148H` -- arises once the Council has dealt with the complaint

GIVEN `what the Council did` IS A `What the Council did`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is a notifiable outcome` IF
    CONSIDER `what the Council did`
    WHEN `made inquiries about it` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `referred it to the Commission for investigation` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `referred it to the Tribunal` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `referred it to a Committee` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

-- s 145BA(1): the notifiable outcomes are (a) the result of an inquiry at a Council meeting; (b) "any action resulting
-- from the assessment" after a referral under s 145B(1)(f); (c) a decision under s 145B(1)(g) (counselling), (h)
-- (conciliation, or Division 9 of Part 2 of the Health Care Complaints Act), (i) (another entity) or (j) (no further
-- action). (2): written notice "within 30 days after the outcome". s 148H(1): after an inquiry at a Council meeting the
-- Council must, "within 30 days of making its decision", make a written statement of the decision available to the
-- complainant, with reasons (3). A referral to the Commission for investigation, to the Tribunal or to a Committee, and
-- the Council's own inquiries, are not notifiable outcomes.
@export The Council has dealt with my complaint: by when must it tell me the outcome (ss 145BA, 148H)?
GIVEN `what the Council did` IS A `What the Council did` @desc What the Council did. One of: "dealt with it by inquiry at a Council meeting", "took action after referring the practitioner for a health or performance assessment or to an Impaired Registrants Panel", "directed the practitioner to attend counselling", "referred it to the Commission for conciliation, or under Division 9 of Part 2 of the Health Care Complaints Act 1993", "referred it to another entity, such as a National Board", "decided that no further action should be taken", "made inquiries about it", "referred it to the Commission for investigation", "referred it to the Tribunal", "referred it to a Committee"
      `the day of the outcome` IS A DATE @desc The day of the outcome: the Council's decision, or the action it took after the assessment (ISO date, e.g. 2026-03-02)
GIVETH AN `Outcome notice`
`When must the Council tell me the outcome` MEANS
    IF (NOT `is a notifiable outcome` `what the Council did`)
    THEN `section 145BA does not require the Council to tell me of this step`
    ELSE IF `what the Council did` EQUALS `dealt with it by inquiry at a Council meeting`
         THEN `the Council must give me written notice of the result, and a written statement of the decision with reasons, by` `the 30th day`
         ELSE `the Council must give me written notice of the outcome by` `the 30th day`
    WHERE
        `the 30th day` MEANS DATE_FROM_SERIAL (DATE_SERIAL `the day of the outcome` PLUS 30)

@export Did the Council tell me the outcome of my complaint in time, or is it overdue (s 145BA(2))?
GIVEN `what the Council did` IS A `What the Council did` @desc What the Council did (as in the question "by when must the Council tell me the outcome")
      `the day of the outcome` IS A DATE @desc The day of the outcome (ISO date)
      `I have been given written notice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Council has given you written notice of the outcome
      `the day I was given notice` IS A DATE @desc If so, the day you were given it (ISO date; any date if not)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date (ISO date)
GIVETH AN `Outcome notice timing`
`Was I told the outcome in time` MEANS
    IF (NOT `is a notifiable outcome` `what the Council did`)
    THEN `section 145BA does not require notice of this step`
    ELSE IF `I have been given written notice`
         THEN IF `outcome notice given in time` (DATE_SERIAL `the day I was given notice` MINUS DATE_SERIAL `the day of the outcome`)
              THEN `given in time`
              ELSE `given late: more than 30 days after the outcome`
         ELSE IF (DATE_SERIAL `today` MINUS DATE_SERIAL `the day of the outcome`) AT MOST 30
              THEN `not yet given, and not yet due`
              ELSE `not yet given, and overdue`

§§ `At the hearing -- ss 147E, 148D, 165J, 171, 171B` -- arises once the complaint is referred

@export A Professional Standards Committee will hold an inquiry into my complaint: was I given enough notice (s 171(4))?
GIVEN `the day I was given notice` IS A DATE @desc The day you were given notice of the inquiry (ISO date)
      `the day of the inquiry` IS A DATE @desc The day the inquiry is to be held (ISO date)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Was I given enough notice of the inquiry` MEANS
    `given enough notice of the inquiry` `the day I was given notice` `the day of the inquiry`

@export Can I attend and have a lawyer at the hearing of my complaint (ss 147E, 148D, 165J, 171B)?
GIVEN `the body` IS A `Complaint body` @desc The body hearing your complaint. One of: "the Tribunal", "a Professional Standards Committee", "an Assessment Committee", "the Council at an inquiry at a Council meeting"
GIVETH A `Representation`
`Can I have a lawyer at the hearing` MEANS
    `representation before the body` `the body` `the complainant`

@export Can I be ordered to pay someone's costs if my complaint is heard (Schedule 5D cl 13)?
GIVEN `the body` IS A `Complaint body` @desc The body hearing your complaint. One of: "the Tribunal", "a Professional Standards Committee", "an Assessment Committee", "the Council at an inquiry at a Council meeting"
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I be ordered to pay costs` MEANS
    `may be ordered to pay costs` `the body`

§§ `What can happen to the practitioner -- ss 146C, 146D, 148E-148G, 149B, 149C` -- arises once the complaint is proved or admitted

@export What is the most the practitioner can be fined over my complaint (ss 146C, 148F, 149B)? Answers in dollars; 0 where the Act bars a fine.
GIVEN `the profession` IS A `Health profession` @desc The practitioner's profession. One of: "medical", "nursing and midwifery", "pharmacy", "another registered health profession"
      `the body` IS A `Complaint body` @desc The body deciding. One of: "the Tribunal", "a Professional Standards Committee", "an Assessment Committee", "the Council at an inquiry at a Council meeting"
      `the finding` IS A `What was found` @desc What the body found, or the practitioner admitted in writing. One of: "unsatisfactory professional conduct", "professional misconduct", "another ground (competence, impairment, a conviction, suitability)", "nothing proved or admitted"
      `no other order is appropriate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the body is satisfied that no other order, or combination of orders, is appropriate in the public interest
      `a court has already fined or penalised the conduct` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a court has already imposed a fine or other penalty for the same conduct
      `dollars per penalty unit` IS A NUMBER @desc The value of a penalty unit in dollars (set by s 17 of the Crimes (Sentencing Procedure) Act 1999; 110 at the time of writing)
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`What is the most the practitioner can be fined` MEANS
    `maximum fine in dollars` `the profession` `the body` `the finding` `no other order is appropriate` `a court has already fined or penalised the conduct` `dollars per penalty unit`

-- s 149C(1): the Tribunal "may suspend ... or cancel ... if the Tribunal is satisfied (a) the practitioner is not
-- competent ...; or (b) ... is guilty of professional misconduct; or (c) ... has been convicted of or made the subject of
-- a criminal finding for an offence ... and the circumstances of the offence render the practitioner unfit in the
-- public interest to practise ...; or (d) ... is not a suitable person for registration". (3): it "must cancel" if
-- satisfied the practitioner "has contravened a critical compliance order or condition". ss 146D(1), 148G(1): a
-- Professional Standards Committee, or a Council at a meeting, "may recommend" suspension or cancellation to the
-- Tribunal if satisfied the practitioner "does not have sufficient physical or mental capacity to practise".
@export Can the practitioner's registration be suspended or cancelled over my complaint (ss 146D, 148G, 149C)?
GIVEN `contravened a critical compliance order or condition` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal is satisfied the practitioner breached an order or condition earlier made "critical compliance" (one whose breach means cancellation)
      `not competent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal is satisfied the practitioner is not competent to practise
      `guilty of professional misconduct` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal is satisfied the practitioner is guilty of professional misconduct
      `convicted, and unfit to practise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal is satisfied the practitioner was convicted of (or found guilty of) an offence whose circumstances make them unfit in the public interest to practise
      `not a suitable person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal is satisfied the practitioner is not a suitable person for registration
      `lacks physical or mental capacity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a Professional Standards Committee or the Council (at a meeting) is satisfied the practitioner lacks sufficient physical or mental capacity to practise
GIVETH A `Suspension or cancellation`
`Can the registration be suspended or cancelled` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `contravened a critical compliance order or condition` THEN `the Tribunal must cancel the registration`
           IF `not competent` OR `guilty of professional misconduct` OR `convicted, and unfit to practise` OR `not a suitable person`
           THEN `the Tribunal may suspend or cancel the registration`
           IF `lacks physical or mental capacity` THEN `a Committee or the Council may recommend suspension or cancellation to the Tribunal`
           OTHERWISE `the Act gives no power to suspend or cancel on these findings`

-- s 149C(5): "If the Tribunal suspends or cancels a registered health practitioner's ... registration and it is satisfied
-- the person poses a substantial risk to the health of members of the public, it may by order (a prohibition order) (a)
-- prohibit the person from providing health services or specified health services ...; (b) place specified conditions
-- on the provision of health services". (5A): also where the person is no longer registered and the Tribunal decides it
-- would have suspended or cancelled the registration.
@export Can the Tribunal stop the practitioner providing health services altogether, by a prohibition order (s 149C(5), (5A))?
GIVEN `the Tribunal suspends or cancels, or would have` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal suspends or cancels the registration (or, for a former practitioner, decides it would have)
      `substantial risk to public health` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal is satisfied the practitioner poses a substantial risk to the health of members of the public
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the practitioner be prohibited from providing health services` MEANS
    JUST (`the Tribunal suspends or cancels, or would have` AND `substantial risk to public health`)

-- s 148E(1)(b): at an inquiry at a Council meeting, the Council may "make an order for the withholding or refunding of
-- part or all of the payment with respect to the fees to be charged or paid for the services that are the subject of
-- the complaint". s 148: the Subdivision "does not apply to a complaint about a health practitioner ... registered in
-- (a) the medical profession; or (b) the nursing and midwifery profession." No other body's list of powers (ss 146B,
-- 149A) includes a refund; those lists are not closed, so the answer there is NOTHING.
@export Can the fees I paid for the treatment be ordered withheld or refunded through my complaint (s 148E(1)(b))?
GIVEN `the profession` IS A `Health profession` @desc The practitioner's profession. One of: "medical", "nursing and midwifery", "pharmacy", "another registered health profession"
      `the body` IS A `Complaint body` @desc The body dealing with the complaint. One of: "the Tribunal", "a Professional Standards Committee", "an Assessment Committee", "the Council at an inquiry at a Council meeting"
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can my fees be ordered refunded` MEANS
    IF `the body` EQUALS `the Council at an inquiry at a Council meeting`
    THEN JUST (NOT (`is a relevant health practitioner for a Professional Standards Committee` `the profession`))
    ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind: no refund power is listed for this body; its list of powers is not closed

§§ `Appeals -- ss 158, 158A, 161` -- arises once a Committee has decided

@export My complaint went to a Committee: can I appeal to the Tribunal against its finding or orders (s 158)?
GIVEN `the body` IS A `Complaint body` @desc The body that decided. One of: "a Professional Standards Committee", "an Assessment Committee", "the Tribunal", "the Council at an inquiry at a Council meeting"
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I appeal against the Committee's decision` MEANS
    `may appeal against the Committee` `the body`

@export Is my appeal to the Tribunal in time (s 161)?
GIVEN `the day I was given written notice of the reasons` IS A DATE @desc The day you were given written notice of the reasons for the decision you are appealing (ISO date)
      `the day I lodged the appeal` IS A DATE @desc The day you lodged (or will lodge) your appeal (ISO date)
      `the Tribunal allowed a longer period` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal has allowed you a longer period
GIVETH AN `Appeal timing`
`Is my appeal in time` MEANS
    `timing of an appeal` `the day I was given written notice of the reasons` `the day I lodged the appeal` `the Tribunal allowed a longer period`

@export By when must I appeal to the Tribunal (s 161(a))? The last day, 28 days after you were given written notice of the reasons.
GIVEN `the day I was given written notice of the reasons` IS A DATE @desc The day you were given written notice of the reasons for the decision (ISO date)
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I appeal` MEANS
    `last day to appeal` `the day I was given written notice of the reasons`

@export Can I appeal to the Tribunal now on a point of law in a Committee inquiry into my complaint (s 158A(2))?
GIVEN `the stage` IS A `Stage of the Committee inquiry` @desc Where the inquiry is. One of: "before notice of the inquiry was given", "after notice of the inquiry, before it began", "during the inquiry", "after the inquiry was completed"
      `the day of the Committee's decision on the point` IS A DATE @desc During the inquiry: the day the Committee decided the point of law (ISO date; any date otherwise)
      `the day I lodge the appeal` IS A DATE @desc The day you lodge (or will lodge) the appeal (ISO date)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I appeal on a point of law now` MEANS
    `point of law appeal may be made` `the stage` (DATE_SERIAL `the day I lodge the appeal` MINUS DATE_SERIAL `the day of the Committee's decision on the point`)

§§ `My civil claim -- ss 145G, 147B(3), 147C(7), 148I, 150(6A), 150A(6), 150B(2)`

-- s 148I: "A finding of a Council under this Subdivision is admissible as evidence in any legal proceedings."
-- s 147B(3): advice an Assessment Committee obtains "may not, unless otherwise ordered by the Council, be admitted or
-- used in civil proceedings before a court and a person may not be compelled to produce the advice or to give evidence
-- in relation to the advice in civil proceedings". s 147C(7): "A person cannot be required in civil proceedings in any
-- court to produce or permit access to a report made to a Committee under this section or to divulge the contents".
-- ss 150(6A), 150A(6), 150B(2): the Council's written reasons for urgent action, for a review of it, and the recording or
-- transcript of the meeting, are "not admissible in evidence in (a) civil or criminal proceedings in a court of law,
-- other than proceedings under this Law; or (b) an inquest or inquiry under the Coroners Act 2009".
@export Can this finding or document from the complaints process be used in my civil claim against the practitioner (ss 147B(3), 147C(7), 148I, 150(6A), 150A(6), 150B(2))?
GIVEN `the document` IS A `Document from the complaint` @desc What you want to use. One of: "a finding of a Council at an inquiry at a Council meeting", "advice an Assessment Committee obtained", "a skills-testing report to an Assessment Committee", "the Council's written reasons for urgent suspension or conditions (s 150)", "the Council's written reasons on a review of urgent action (s 150A)", "an audio recording or transcript of a Council meeting about urgent action (s 150B)", "another document or finding"
      `the Council ordered it may be used` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For Assessment Committee advice: whether the Council has ordered that it may be admitted or used
GIVETH A `Use in my civil claim`
`Can it be used in my civil claim` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the document`
    WHEN `a finding of a Council at an inquiry at a Council meeting` THEN `admissible as evidence in any legal proceedings`
    WHEN `advice an Assessment Committee obtained` THEN
        IF `the Council ordered it may be used`
        THEN `usable, because the Council has ordered otherwise`
        ELSE `not admissible in my civil claim, and no one can be made to produce it or give evidence about it`
    WHEN `a skills-testing report to an Assessment Committee` THEN `no one can be required to produce it or reveal its contents in civil proceedings`
    WHEN `the Council's written reasons for urgent suspension or conditions (s 150)` THEN `not admissible in court proceedings (other than under the National Law) or at an inquest`
    WHEN `the Council's written reasons on a review of urgent action (s 150A)` THEN `not admissible in court proceedings (other than under the National Law) or at an inquest`
    WHEN `an audio recording or transcript of a Council meeting about urgent action (s 150B)` THEN `not admissible in court proceedings (other than under the National Law) or at an inquest`
    WHEN `another document or finding` THEN `the Act does not say`
